## 1. Introduction
Recently, controllable integration of two or more functional components into advanced materials to obtain composite materials with collective properties and improved performance has attracted a great deal of research attention. As an example, core– shell or core–shell-like nanoparticle $@$ metal–organic-framework $(\mathrm{NP}@\mathrm{MOF})$ nanostructures are believed to be one of the most convenient and effective ways to achieve property synergies of inorganic NPs and MOFs for multifunctional applications. On the one hand, because of the MOF shell coating, the migration and aggregation of the NP cores with small size and high surface energy can be dramatically limited, so that the stability and chemical activity of the NP cores are preserved. 1  On the other hand, the advantages of both the NP cores (unique optical, electrical, magnetic, and catalytic properties) and the MOF shells (structural adaptivity and flexibility, ordered crystalline pores, and multi-coordination sites) can be combined effectively and, therefore, new physical and chemical properties that are not possible from single-component materials may be accessed. 2 Thanks to their structural distinctiveness and property diversity, $\mathsf{N P}@\mathsf{M O F}$ core–shell nanostructures have demonstrated many application potentials in gas storage/separation, 3–6  catalysis, 6–11  sensing, 12,13  etc. 8,14,15
