## 2. Single-Component-NP@MOF Core–Shell Nanostructures
The design and construction of any complex multicomponent system should start with the simplest ones. For $\mathsf{N P}@\mathsf{M O F}$ core–shell nanostructures, the currently prepared ones are based mainly on single-component inorganic NP cores, including metal NPs, 6,7  metal oxide NPs and nanorods (NRs), 8,14–16  etc. Generally, most have been synthesized via the following three methods: i) using MOFs as templates, small and naked NPs are generated and embedded in the cavities or channels of MOFs; 1c 3d 6 7 17  ii) the presynthesized stabilizer-capped NPs are encapsulated within the MOF layers; 7,8,12,18  and iii) the NP cores are synthesized together with the MOF shells in a one-pot reaction. 13  Moreover, it has been found that those single-component $\mathrm{NP}@\mathrm{MOF}$ core–shell nanostructures exhibit excellent catalytic, sensing, or magnetic properties.
## 2.1. Metal-NP@MOF Core–Shell Nanostructures
Incorporating metal NPs within MOFs is an important means to enhance the materials’ performance, especially for catalysis and gas storage. 19  A rapidly increasing number of publications demonstrate the potential of “metal-NP $@$ MOF” systems (the embedding metal NPs mainly include Pd, Au, Ru, Cu, Pt, Ni, and Ag) for applications in these two areas. Several excellent reviews have been published concerning the preparation methodologies, characterization techniques, and applications of the metal-NP ${\ @\mathrm{MOF}}$ core–shell nanostructures in catalysis and gas storage; 6 7  therefore, the related content will not be further described here.
Besides applications in catalysis and gas storage, metalNP@MOF core–shell nanostructures are candidates for highly sensitive molecule detection, integrating the metal NP cores of the surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) activity and the MOF shells of the selective enrichment target in the composite nanostructures. 12,13,20  For example, Sada et al. reported that composite crystals, which were prepared by direct growth of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(4,4^{\prime}$ -biphenyldicarboxilate) $_3]$ MOF on the surface of 11-mercaptoundecanoic acid-coated Au NRs, exhibited a high SERS response toward guest molecules such as N,N-diethylformamide and $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ 12  Very recently, we prepared $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{MOF}{\cdot}5$ $\{\mathrm{MOF}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{5}~=~[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}]_{n}$ bdc $^{\circ}=1,4$ -terephthalate} core–shell nanostructures and investigated their application in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ detection. 13  Interestingly, the $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{MOF}{-}5$ core–shell nanostructures with an ideal shell thickness of $(3.2\pm0.5)\$ nm were found to possess the highest SERS activity toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in gas mixtures (Figure 1). 13  The shell thickness-dependent sensing characteristics were attributed to both the near-field electromagnetic enhancement of the Au NP cores (mostly within a distance of no more than $3\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) and the specific adsorption capability of the MOF-5 shells toward the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, 21  revealing that accurate control over the composite core–shell nanostructures is significant for their practical applications. Impressively, such sensitive SERS detection could be easily applied to other analytes, including dimethylformamide and ethanol by using the $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{MOF}{\cdot}5$ and $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{ZIF}-8$ core–shell nanostructures, respectively. 13
## 2.2. Metal-Oxide-NP@MOF Core–Shell Nanostructures
In principle, the preparation recipes of metal-oxide-NP ${\overline{{\ @\mathrm{MOF}}}}$ core–shell nanostructures should be similar to that of metal$\mathrm{NP}@\mathrm{MOF}$ core–shell nanostructures. However, up to now, only a few metal-oxide-NP ${\overline{{\ @\mathrm{MOF}}}}$ core–shell nanostructures have been reported. 8,14–16  One of the earliest examples was successful incorporation of magnetic iron oxide particles into porous MOFs, 14a  which not only provided opportunity for external manipulation of highly microporous MOFs for efficient catalyst separation but also realized the controllable release of drugs triggered by magnetically induced heating. After that, controllable synthesis of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}$ (benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate) $_{2}]$ $\{\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]\}$ core–shell magnetic microspheres with strong magnetic properties was achieved by a step-by-step assembly strategy (Figure 2), and the structure, composition, and function of these microspheres could be easily tailored by choosing various metal ions and functional organic ligands or tuning the assembly processes. 14b  A big advantage of the metal-oxide-NP ${\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\mathit{\sigma}}}}({\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{OF}}}$ core–shell nanostructures is that, after the coating process, their saturation magnetization is still high enough for efficient separation using existing magnetic technology. For example, produced $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}$ core–shell NPs with a strong magnetic property were used as a high-pressure liquid chromatography medium for separation of toluene and $p$ -xylene. 14c  It should be pointed out that although the magnetic- ${\mathrm{JP}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ core–shell nanostructures hold a huge potential for applications in catalysis and chromatographic separation, as well as drug delivery, due to the combination of the selectivity of the MOF shells and the magnetic response of the NP cores, work on their capability for applications has been scarcely reported to date, suggesting that long-term efforts should be taken and there is lots of work to be done.
In addition to zero-dimensional NPs, one-dimensional metal oxide NRs might be combined with MOFs to generate core–shell nanostructures. Up to now, only two core–shell nanostructures based on ZnO NRs have been successfully fabricated. 15  The first is growth of nanoscale MOF-5 crystallines onto ordered ZnO NR arrays using a slow-diffusion approach combined with molecular self-assembly technology, so that ZnO ${\mathrm{NR}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}{\cdot}5$ hybrid films with core–shell structures were formed. 15a  The photoluminescence spectrum of the ZnO $\mathrm{NR}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ hybrid films displayed a blue shift in green emission accompanying an intensity reduction in UV emission compared to pure ZnO NR arrays, disclosing the application potential of such core–shell NR arrays in sensors, micro/nanodevices, and screen displays. 15a  Another example is the ZnO NR $@$ zeolitic-imidazolate-frameworks-8 (ZIF-8) core–shell nanostructures (Figure 3a), which were fabricated via a self-template strategy where $\mathrm{znO}$ NRs not only act as the cores but also provide $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions for formation of the ZIF-8 shells. 15b  Notably, the as-prepared ZnO NR $@$ ZIF-8 core–shell nanorods exhibited distinct photoelectrochemical response to hole scavengers of different molecular sizes (e.g., $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and ascorbic acid (AA)). Seen from Figure 3b, it was obvious that the photocurrent increased with the concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ added, whereas the photocurrent only showed a slight alternation with the concentration of AA added. The different photoelectrochemical responses were assigned to the pore size of the ZIF-8 shells (about $\mathring{3.4}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\big]$ , which was larger than that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ but smaller than that of AA, allowing for selective penetration of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ analytes (Figure 3c). Therefore, it is reasonable to deduce that such semiconductor N $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{\mathrm{R}}@\mathrm{{MOF}}}$ core–shell heterostructures could be constructed for many types of optical/electric sensors with high sensitivity and specific selectivity, if the NR cores and the MOF shells are judiciously chosen.
In addition to the above two categories, some other inorganic or even polymer NPs have been introduced into the MOF systems to construct single-component-N ${\bf\boldsymbol{\?}}@\mathrm{{MOF}}$ core–shell nanostructures with unique functions for applications in wide areas, such as quantum-dot $@$ MOFs for photoluminescence, 8 metal-complex@MOFs for catalysis, 11,22  carbon-nanotube $@$ MOFs for gas storage/separation, 4  metal-hydride $@$ MOFs for hydrogen storage, 5  and polystyrene $@$ MOFs hybrid photonic films for optics. 23  All these results highlight that the limitless choice of the NP cores and the MOF shells provide endless possibilities for function manipulation and further application.
## 3. Two-or-More-Component-NP@MOF Core–Shell Nanostructures
Extended synthesis of core–shell nanostructures from singlecomponent NP cores to NP cores of two or more components is intriguing for advanced applications. NP components with different functions could have significant and strong coupling effects at the nanometer scale, giving rise not only to remarkable enhancement of the intrinsic performance but also to generation of new functions absent in the single-componentNP-core systems. Thus, applying composite NPs as the cores of the ${\mathrm{NP}}\ @{\mathrm{MOF}}$ core–shell nanostructures will be an important research topic for this type of nanomaterial.
Design and construction of the two-or-more-component$\mathsf{N P}@\mathsf{M O F}$ core–shell nanostructures for potential applications remain at a nascent stage, and, to our knowledge, only a few examples have been reported. 1b 3c 8 10 16a 24 25 One of the most difficult challenges is how to effectively prevent uncontrollable agglomeration of two or more types of NPs, so that they can be dispersed in an orderly way within MOFs, while also preserving their properties. One plausible solution is the introduction of some surfactants, like polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), into the synthesis systems of the NP $@$ MOF core–shell nanostructures as surface capping agents, so as to increase their thermodynamic stability in polar solvents (such as methanol and N,N-dimethylformamide, commonly used as solvents for MOF synthesis). 7,8,26  By using this strategy, different types of PVPcapped inorganic NPs with varying sizes, shapes, and compositions were successfully incorporated within crystals of a readily synthesized MOF material, ZIF-8, in a well-dispersed fashion (Figure 4a). 8  Impressively, the physical characteristics of different NP cores were well maintained after encapsulation of the ZIF-8 shells (Figure 4b,c).
The study of the two-or-more-component-N $\mathsf{P}@\mathrm{MOF}$ core– shell nanostructures has been mostly focused on the selection and preparation of bimetallic NPs as the cores, 1b 3c 8 24  because compared with single-component NPs, their catalytic activities are much higher owing to the strong synergistic effect. 1b 3c Similar interest has also been paid to the metal/metal-oxide $@$ MOF core–shell nanostructures. 10 16a 25  For instance, the initial catalytic productivity of the $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ core–shell structures for methanol synthesis was found to outperform the productivity of singly loaded $\operatorname{Cu}@\operatorname{MOF}{\cdot}5$ and peak at about $60\%$ of an industrial reference catalyst. Their high catalytic activity originated from the comparably low Cu loading $(1.4~\mathrm{wt\%})$ and the small Cu specific surface area (less than $1\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ compared to the $\operatorname{Cu}@\operatorname{MOF}{\cdot}5$ samples ( $13.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Cu; $65~\mathrm{{m}^{2}~\mathrm{{g}^{-1}~C u)}}$ , suggesting a very efficient interfacial contact between the Cu and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ NPs in the $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ core–shell structures. 10 16a Unfortunately, after repeated catalytic cycles, both $z\mathrm{nO}$ and
Cu within MOF-5 were sintered, and meanwhile the MOF-5 matrix was also collapsed, leading to poor final activities. 10 Thus, there is a long way to go in order to achieve their practical applications.
## 4. Conclusion
Taking into account the novelty of ${\mathrm{NP}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ core-shell nanostructures and the recent interest in exploiting their functions via incorporating a variety of inorganic NPs into MOFs, there is no doubt that this field will continue to attract much attention in the future. Seen from the significant progress described above, it is clear that this emerging type of core–shell nanostructured materials represents significant opportunities as well as providing major challenges in many areas of research. i) The development of general preparative methods for well-defined $\mathrm{NP}@$ MOF core–shell nanostructures is highly desirable. The synthesis difficulty lies in the fact that crystalline MOFs prefer selfnucleation in solution due to possible lattice mismatch between the MOF and NP materials. Therefore, how to realize controllable growth of MOFs on the surface of NPs while keeping the size, shape, and dispersibility of NPs will be primary research topics in the future. ii) Both the types and arrangements of NPs and MOFs in the nanostructures should be extended. In particular, to date, most cores inside the ${\mathrm{NP}}\ @{\mathrm{MOF}}$ core–shell nanostructures are inorganic NPs, and scarce examples are fabricated on the basis of organic NPs though they have many applications in devices and biology. Furthermore, as-achieved products are mostly solid core–shell nanostructures, and others of the distinctive ${\mathrm{NP}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ nanostructures, like yolk-shelled or multi-shelled hollow nanospheres, are worthy of study. iii) The properties of the ${\mathrm{NP}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ core–shell nanostructures need to be deeply investigated. For example, could the conformation of the chiral pores in the MOF shells be effective for selective enrichment of enantiomers and facilitate the chiral catalysis implemented by the NP cores? Could the photogenerated electrons and holes be separated for photocatalytic hydrogen generation if semiconducting NPs and MOFs are integrated in an orderly way into the core–shell nanostructures? iv) The multifunctional applications of the ${\mathrm{NP}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ core–shell nanostructures are waiting to be explored, though they have been broadly recognized. In the catalysis application, the $\mathsf{N P}@\mathsf{M O F}$ core– shell nanostructures will be good candidates for catalyzing cascade reactions with high activity and selectivity if catalytic NPs and MOFs are combined, while in the biomedicine field, the ${\mathrm{NP}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ core–shell nanostructures are expected to be next generation of theranostic agents, since inorganic NP cores and porous MOF shells could be used for optical imaging and drug delivery, respectively.
In conclusion, ${\mathrm{NP}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ is becoming the forth major type of core–shell nanostructure after $\mathrm{NP}@$ polymer, $\mathrm{NP}@$ silica, and $\mathrm{NP}@$ metal oxide. 27  With respect to the previous three types of core–shell nanostructures, the outstanding advantages of $\mathrm{NP}@$ MOF, such as limitless selection of composition, tunable pore sizes of the shells, and multifunctional cores and shells, will provide an unprecedented opportunity to manipulate the material properties at the nanometer scale. We highly expect that the research enthusiasm on NP $@$ MOF core–shell nanostructures will lead to many exciting applications in devices, energy, the environment, and medicine.
